## Correlation of the indices

::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="R code: Correlation of the indices"}

::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code .hidden}
#| label: feasibility-blocking-correlation
# Per-participant original index (BI2 on the first test block) against the
# alternative index (BI3 over all test blocks), with Pearson's r and its 95% CI
# in each analysis set
index_pairs <- dplyr::inner_join(
  dplyr::select(indices_first, id, bi2 = bi_confidence),
  dplyr::select(indices_pooled, id, bi3 = bi_choice),
  by = "id"
)

index_cors <- purrr::map(sets, \(ids) {
  pairs <- dplyr::filter(index_pairs, id %in% ids)
  stats::cor.test(pairs$bi2, pairs$bi3, method = "pearson")
})

# In-text values: r and its CI without the leading zero
index_cor_text <- purrr::map_chr(index_cors, \(test) {
  values <- sub(
    "^(-?)0\\.",
    "\\1.",
    sprintf("%.2f", c(test$estimate, test$conf.int))
  )
  sprintf("*r* = %s, 95%% CI [%s, %s]", values[1L], values[2L], values[3L])
})
```
:::

:::

Both indices are computed from the same test stage, the original one from the
confidence-weighted choices of the first test block, the alternative one from
the choices of all test blocks. Per participant, they correlated as follows:

- **Full sample:** *r* = .82, 95% CI [.55, .94],
- **Learned (primary) set:** *r* = .87, 95% CI [.54, .97],
- **Naive set:** *r* = .88, 95% CI [.46, .98].

